  "title": "A Late Upload Flipped a Georgia House Race. The Hand Count Confirmed It.",
  "deck": "Republican Gerald Greene won reelection after Seminole County corrected its vote totals. Georgia certified the election on Nov. 20, and the state is still investigating why hundreds of votes were left out of the initial tally.",
  "category": "politics",
  "categoryLabel": "U.S. Politics",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-25T12:11:31Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-25T12:11:31Z",
  "body": [
    "Georgia has certified its election results, and a hand count in Seminole County confirmed the corrected tally in a state House race that changed hands in the unofficial returns. The question still facing state investigators is how several hundred Election Day votes went unreported long enough to put the wrong candidate ahead.",
    "Republican incumbent Gerald Greene initially trailed Democratic challenger Harry James in House District 154. Greene took the lead after Seminole County uploaded results that had been left out of its initial count. He went on to win reelection.",
    "The Carter Center, which observed the county's full hand count for the race, said Monday that the count confirmed Seminole County's certified results. That same day, the Georgia Secretary of State's Office told WALB that its investigation was still open and would be referred to the State Election Board once complete.",
    "## How officials found the gap",
    "During its post-election review, the secretary of state's office found about 388 more Election Day voter check-ins in Seminole County than ballots reported cast. State officials alerted the county, which traced the discrepancy to results from a memory card that had not been properly uploaded into its election management system.",
    "Greene had trailed by 58 votes, according to The Atlanta Journal-Constitution. After the upload, the secretary of state's office reported that he led by 262. Those figures describe the shift in the unofficial count as it stood Nov. 6.",
    "Seminole County Elections Supervisor Ron Foskey said the physical card had been accounted for and the problem was with how its results were processed. The county requested a full hand count of the District 154 ballots cast in Seminole County, which the Carter Center observed.",
    "## What the hand count settled",
    "Greene told WALB the correction showed Georgia's election safeguards worked. James said voters were still asking why the state certified the results while the investigation was open. He also asked how adding the votes affected other races on Seminole County's ballot.",
    "The hand count answered whether the corrected county tally matched the paper ballots. The state's investigation is examining why the results were left out of the first upload, whether required procedures were followed, and what should change before the next election. The secretary of state's office has given no timetable for its findings."
  ],
